WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.071 --> 00:00:04.024
(lightning booming)

00:00:04.024 --> 00:00:05.850
- There was a great thing that somebody

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:07.020
said to me years ago,

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.100
and I say it all the time.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:12.040
There's two ways to build
the biggest building in town.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:15.200
You either just build the
biggest building in town,

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.710
or you spend all your time

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.773
tearing everybody else's building down.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:22.330
And if there's anything on my last breath

00:00:22.330 --> 00:00:24.160
that I'm most proud of, is I'm out here

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.610
just trying to build the biggest building.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.820
It would be the best if all eight billion

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.230
people had this mindset.

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.266
You wouldn't have a single
bad thing happen in society.

00:00:32.266 --> 00:00:34.570
(laughs)

00:00:34.570 --> 00:00:36.110
If you actually cared about everybody else

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:39.900
and didn't impose your
own hurt on someone else.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:40.930
- [Interviewer] So where do we start?

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:42.430
Because you would say parents, maybe?

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.380
- Yeah, I think you start
with even the conversation.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.245
We start with this fucking film.

00:00:46.245 --> 00:00:48.745
(urban music)

00:00:59.176 --> 00:01:03.600
I think about succeeding
in one very simple story.

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:05.864
There are two ways, my friends, to build

00:01:05.864 --> 00:01:07.923
the biggest building in town.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.203
Step number one, just build
the biggest building in town.

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.040
Step number two, build
a decent-sized building

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:20.630
and then spend all your
time trying to tear down

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:23.070
everybody else's building around you down,

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.410
so you end up being the
biggest building in town.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:30.100
My friend, 95% of people
try to do number two.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.158
People that tear everyone
else's building down

00:01:32.158 --> 00:01:34.440
are hurting inside and want other people

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.814
to feel pain, misery loves company.

00:01:36.814 --> 00:01:39.630
And are so insecure
that they actually think

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:44.630
that that $1 or that
opportunity is only available.

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.560
They don't live in a world of abundance.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:50.310
I do think some people think
the lives of a population,

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.240
like black lives matter more,

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.550
that means my life matters less.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:56.320
And I think that's super wrong.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:00.000
That's why people are replying
saying all lives matter.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.260
They don't get it.

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.750
They think that it's like a competition

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:07.190
or there's space that
black lives are taking up.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:08.700
People hear black privilege.

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:10.250
And I've been watching how
people have been acting.

00:02:10.250 --> 00:02:11.620
They think that comes at the cost

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:13.140
of somebody who's white or Asian.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.200
- Absolutely.
- That's crazy.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:16.930
This is not a black,
white, girl, boy issue.

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:19.063
This is a positive, negative issue.

00:02:19.063 --> 00:02:19.992
- [Man] That's it.

00:02:19.992 --> 00:02:21.320
- It's fucking stupid.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.800
Of course your life matters too.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.980
How do you not understand that you should

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.720
only be as happy as your
most unhappy fellow human.

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:32.530
That level of compassion,
sympathy, empathy

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:35.720
is fucking required
for your own happiness.

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.660
How in the world does anybody translate

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:41.000
black lives matter in their head as

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.590
no, no, but my life matters too.

00:02:42.590 --> 00:02:43.933
Yes, yes, yes.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:47.889
But that's why this is on my mind,

00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:50.823
because when you see
things happen socially,

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:53.938
it translates in my brain business-wise.

00:02:53.938 --> 00:02:55.690
These are the same people that think

00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.297
when somebody else's store opens up,

00:02:57.297 --> 00:02:59.560
that they're gonna lose.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.380
See, I think the problem is so many people

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.940
want to win at somebody else's expense

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.020
without understanding that there's so many

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.260
variations of winning, and every one of us

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.270
has a totally different definition for it.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.560
And when my homie Travis from Uber,

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.130
and when my homie, Chris
Saka becomes the best VC,

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:17.290
and goes on "Shark Tank" and they beat me,

00:03:17.290 --> 00:03:19.250
I'm the most pumped for them.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.250
They deserve it.

00:03:20.250 --> 00:03:22.503
And then I say, but it's not over yet.

00:03:22.503 --> 00:03:23.500
(audience laughing)

00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:24.760
And that's who I am.

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.580
- [Interviewer] Why do
some people get mad?

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:27.450
- Because they're sad.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.790
Because they're selfish.

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:30.160
They care about themselves.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:31.950
But the question is, why do
they care about themselves?

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:34.080
They care about themselves
because they're insecure,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.840
and it's a protective mechanism.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:37.750
Most people that come along that have

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:41.027
a lot of talent to amass attention

00:03:41.027 --> 00:03:45.993
and resources and wealth and power,

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:52.470
a solid amount of them
lack humility and altruism.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.930
But I think a lot of
people were also lucky

00:03:55.930 --> 00:03:58.510
in having kindness and capability in them.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.110
They just weren't watching something

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.410
that made them think
that was the thing to do.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.130
That's what this is all about, right?

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:11.030
This is all about like,
no, I'm not that special.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:12.470
There's actually millions of people

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.150
in the last 100 years that had what I had.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:19.470
It's just they grew up in
societies and messaging

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:20.860
that made them think differently.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:25.000
I think that Steve Jobs
came along, became an icon.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.540
But the sad part of that narrative

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:29.730
was he did not treat his employees well.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.090
He became an icon and
the narrative became,

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.770
he got the most out of
people by being a jerk.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.640
And that became romanticized.

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:39.310
I could literally watch people that I knew

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:43.370
who used to be nicer start
treating employees like jerks

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:45.130
because that's what Steve Jobs did.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.330
And a lot of people in
Silicon Valley today

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:49.180
run companies where they're mean

00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:50.650
because they think that's
the right thing to do

00:04:50.650 --> 00:04:52.890
because they put Steve Jobs on a pedestal.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.520
I want my pedestal moment.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:56.180
I want to become that big.

00:04:56.180 --> 00:04:58.060
And what I want to come from that

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:00.240
is that kids that aren't even born today

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:03.360
think that they can build
a $5 billion company

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.400
and be a great guy or great gal.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.980
And what I want to do is
inspire two 14 year old girls

00:05:08.980 --> 00:05:12.600
in Kansas City right now to
build a billion dollar company

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.060
on having a bunch of employees

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.150
hugging each other in the halls.

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:19.150
I feel this crazy, weird
sense of responsibility

00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:21.772
that I was given all this
talent and capability.

00:05:21.772 --> 00:05:26.340
And I'm like, okay, I need
to communicate how I do it,

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.490
because I do think I do
it with a more empathetic,

00:05:30.490 --> 00:05:34.383
sympathetic, compassionate,
human, good intent.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.730
If you asked me point
blank, do I feel like

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.960
I've already moved the
collective entrepreneurial world

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.240
a little bit, a hair towards it?

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:47.030
I do because I've just
had hundreds and hundreds

00:05:47.030 --> 00:05:49.900
and hundreds and
thousands of entrepreneurs

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:52.760
reach out to me and
say, I'm a better person

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:53.763
because of this or that.

00:05:53.763 --> 00:05:56.800
And I think the more successful I am,

00:05:56.800 --> 00:05:59.950
the more people will try
to parrot that behavior.

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:04.270
So I think positivity and
good is practical advice

00:06:04.270 --> 00:06:05.670
to building an empire.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:06.800
And I wanna be the poster
child of the person

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:11.070
that built the biggest, baddest empire

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.070
and did it by being a
good dude along the way.

00:06:14.070 --> 00:06:17.050
And that's the legacy I want to leave.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:19.084
Let me make it perfectly clear to you.

00:06:19.084 --> 00:06:20.970
The world is abundant.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:24.170
Nobody else is taking out of your pocket.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:26.690
Everybody who's watching
this film right now,

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:29.593
if you're good enough,
nothing's gonna stop ya.

